+| `samples` | records the telemetry workload holds | 1024 |
+| `series` | distinct series in it | 8 |
+| `distinct_values` | distinct value bit patterns in it | 16 |
+| `samples_read` | records that came back out of both segments | 2048 |
+| `mismatches_max` | records that did not come back, or came back different | 0 |
+| `column_block_bytes_max` | the telemetry workload's columns, before compression | 3100 |
+| `segment_bytes_max` | the file that holds them | 200 |
+| `bytes_per_sample_max` | the file over the samples, the number this step exists for | 0.20 |
+| `compression_ratio_max` | the file over the column block it came from | 0.07 |
+| `flat_column_block_bytes_max` | one series, one clock, one value, repeated 1024 times | 1150 |
+
+The last five are **upper bounds** rather than targets: they may go down and may not go up, so a
+cheaper encoding — a run-length of the deltas, a dictionary for the values — passes this lane and a
+bigger one fails it. The first five are counts of the workload itself, which is what keeps a small
+file from being an empty one; `mismatches_max` at zero is the round trip, measured by the same run.
 
